### What is the Singleton pattern?

The Singleton pattern ensures a class has exactly one instance across the entire application and provides a global access point to it. The class itself controls its instantiation by making its constructor private and exposing a static method that returns the single instance.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class ConfigManager {
    private static final ConfigManager INSTANCE = new ConfigManager();

    private ConfigManager() {
        // Load configuration from properties file
    }

    public static ConfigManager getInstance() {
        return INSTANCE;
    }

    public String getProperty(String key) {
        // Return configuration value
        return System.getProperty(key, "default");
    }
}
```

### Why does the Singleton pattern exist?

Some resources must be shared and coordinated across the entire application. A database connection pool is the classic example: opening connections is expensive, the pool must be shared so connections are reused, and multiple pools would quickly exhaust the database.

Without a Singleton, any class could create its own connection pool, leading to resource waste and inconsistency. The Singleton guarantees one pool, one configuration, or one logger for the whole process.

```java
// Without Singleton: multiple pools exhaust the database
ConnectionPool pool1 = new ConnectionPool(); // 10 connections
ConnectionPool pool2 = new ConnectionPool(); // 10 more connections
// Now 20 connections instead of 10, and they are uncoordinated
```

### How do you implement Singleton safely in Java?

There are several approaches, each with different trade-offs for thread safety, laziness, and simplicity.

#### Eager initialisation

The instance is created at class-load time. It is thread-safe because the JVM guarantees that static initialisers run exactly once, but it is not lazy.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class ConfigManager {
    private static final ConfigManager INSTANCE = new ConfigManager();
    private ConfigManager() { }
    public static ConfigManager getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }
}
```

#### Initialization-on-demand holder (recommended)

The instance lives in a private static nested class. This is both lazy and thread-safe without any explicit locking, because the JVM loads the holder class only when `getInstance()` is first called, and class loading is synchronised by the JVM.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class ConfigManager {
    private ConfigManager() { }

    private static class Holder {
        static final ConfigManager INSTANCE = new ConfigManager();
    }

    public static ConfigManager getInstance() {
        return Holder.INSTANCE;
    }
}
```

#### Enum singleton (safest)

The enum approach is the simplest and safest. It is immune to reflection attacks and handles serialisation correctly by default.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public enum ConfigManager {
    INSTANCE;

    public String getDbUrl() {
        return "jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/national_bank";
    }
}
```

```java
// Usage
String url = ConfigManager.INSTANCE.getDbUrl();
```

### How does double-checked locking work?

Double-checked locking checks the instance twice: once without a lock and once with a lock. It requires the instance field to be `volatile` so that the write to the field is visible to all threads. Without `volatile`, a thread can see a partially constructed object.

```java
package com.bank.pool;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ConnectionPool {
    private static volatile ConnectionPool instance;

    private ConnectionPool() {
        // Open initial database connections
    }

    public static ConnectionPool getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {                  // First check (no lock)
            synchronized (ConnectionPool.class) {
                if (instance == null) {          // Second check (with lock)
                    instance = new ConnectionPool();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }

    public void executeQuery(String sql) {
        // Borrow connection, execute, return connection
    }
}
```

The holder idiom is preferred because it achieves the same result with less complexity and no chance of forgetting `volatile`.

### What are common mistakes with Singleton?

The first mistake is forgetting `volatile` in double-checked locking. Without it, the JVM may reorder the write to the instance field and the constructor execution, so another thread can see a half-initialised object.

```java
// Wrong: missing volatile
private static ConnectionPool instance; // Another thread can see
                                         // a partially constructed object

// Correct: volatile ensures visibility
private static volatile ConnectionPool instance;
```

The second mistake is not protecting against reflection. A determined caller can use reflection to invoke the private constructor and create a second instance.

```java
// Attack: reflection breaks the Singleton
Constructor<ConfigManager> c = ConfigManager.class
    .getDeclaredConstructor();
c.setAccessible(true);
ConfigManager second = c.newInstance(); // Now there are two instances
```

The enum singleton is immune to this because the JVM prevents reflective construction of enum instances.

### When should you use Singleton?

Use Singleton for genuinely process-wide resources where having more than one instance would cause problems: a connection pool, a configuration manager, or a shared cache. These are expensive to create, must be shared, and must be coordinated.

Do not use Singleton for objects that could reasonably have multiple instances, for objects that need different configurations in tests, or as a lazy alternative to proper dependency injection.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import com.bank.config.ConfigManager;
import com.bank.pool.ConnectionPool;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SingletonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Both calls return the same instance
        ConfigManager config1 = ConfigManager.getInstance();
        ConfigManager config2 = ConfigManager.getInstance();
        System.out.println("Same config? " + (config1 == config2));

        ConnectionPool pool = ConnectionPool.getInstance();
        pool.executeQuery("SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 'SAV2001'");
    }
}
```

```text
Same config? true
```

### How does Singleton interact with dependency injection?

In modern systems, dependency injection frameworks like Spring manage bean scopes. A Spring bean with `@Scope("singleton")` (the default) behaves like a Singleton, with one instance per application context, but it is managed by the framework rather than by the class itself.

This gives the same "one instance" guarantee while keeping the code testable, because in a test you can inject a mock. A true Singleton with a private constructor and a static `getInstance()` method is harder to mock.

```java
// Spring-managed singleton: testable
@Service
public class AccountService {
    private final ConnectionPool pool;

    public AccountService(ConnectionPool pool) {
        this.pool = pool; // Injected, can be mocked in tests
    }
}
```

### What about serialisation?

If a Singleton implements `Serializable`, deserialisation creates a new instance by default, breaking the Singleton guarantee. The fix is to implement `readResolve()` to return the existing instance.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.io.Serializable;

public class ConfigManager implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;
    private static final ConfigManager INSTANCE = new ConfigManager();
    private ConfigManager() { }
    public static ConfigManager getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }

    // Prevent deserialisation from creating a second instance
    private Object readResolve() {
        return INSTANCE;
    }
}
```

The enum singleton handles this automatically, which is another reason it is the safest approach.

### What should a senior engineer know?

Singletons are easy to overuse and become hidden global state. In production systems, Pankaj prefers dependency injection to hand out a single managed instance, which gives the "one instance" benefit while keeping the code testable. A true Singleton is justified only for genuinely process-wide resources like a connection pool.

The holder idiom and the enum singleton are the two recommended approaches. Double-checked locking is error-prone. Eager initialisation is acceptable when the resource is always needed.

```java
// Senior approach: DI-managed singleton with testability
@Configuration
public class BankConfig {
    @Bean
    public ConnectionPool connectionPool() {
        return new ConnectionPool("jdbc:postgresql://localhost/national_bank");
    }
}
// Spring creates exactly one ConnectionPool and injects it everywhere
// In tests, you can @MockBean ConnectionPool for isolation
```
